# What this means

Driftfin is free for personal use. In plain terms:

- You may download the official APKs from GitHub Releases, sideload them, and
  use Driftfin on your own devices.
- You may view the source for transparency, clone it, and build it on your own
  devices for personal use and testing.
- You may fork this repository within GitHub, as GitHub's Terms of Service
  provide for public repositories. That permission covers the fork on GitHub
  itself, not publishing or distributing your own version anywhere else.
- You may modify the source to prepare contributions back to this project
  (see CONTRIBUTING.md). Contributions are merged only at the maintainer's
  discretion.
- Otherwise, you may NOT copy, modify, redistribute, re-publish, re-brand, or
  create derivative works from Driftfin's source or binaries, publish a
  modified or renamed version, or use Driftfin source or binaries in another
  project, without written permission. The Driftfin name and logo may not be
  used to brand another build or product.

Build 24 and earlier were published under the MIT License. That grant is
permanent: anyone who received those builds keeps their MIT rights to that
material, including MIT-licensed material carried forward into later builds.
Only original code and assets first published after build 24 are proprietary;
the MIT grant does not extend to them. The full MIT License text is
reproduced in the appendix below, as that license requires, and is also kept
with those releases and git tags; this notice does not replace it.

This notice covers Driftfin's own original source code and assets only.
Third-party libraries keep their own licenses (see THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md).
Movie and show artwork belongs to its owners and is not licensed by this
notice; it is not part of Driftfin's own branding.

